<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-7" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>3D Vectors</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Three dimensional vectors are similar to two-dimensional (and four or <m>n</m>-dimensional) vectors. You can add and subtract them, find their magnitude and multiply them.
      The notation for three-dimensional vectors is <md>\mathbf{v} = \langle x ,y, x \rangle = x\mathbf{\hat{i}} + y\mathbf{\hat{j}} + z\mathbf{\hat{k}} </md>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Three-dimensional space is defined by the right hand rule (sorry left-handers). In the <m>x</m>-direction left is negative and right is positive and when viewed on
      a two-dimensional plane, positive <m>y</m> is up and negative <m>y</m> is down. To define positive and negative in a third <m>z</m>-direction, we use our right hands.
      Point the fingers on your right hand in the positive <m>x</m>-direction and curl them to the positive <m>y</m>-direction, then the thumb on your right hand is pointing 
      in the positive <m>z</m>-direction. If the <m>x-y</m> plane is drawn as usual on a white/chalk board, then the positive <m>z</m>-direction points out of the board.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="right-hand-rule">
      <caption>The right-hand rule</caption>
      <image source="right-hand-rule.png" width="100%">
        <description>

      <p>
        An image with two figures. The figure on the left shows a three-dimensional axes. The figure on the right has a hand demonstrating the right-hand rule with a three
        dimensional axes superimposed over the hand.
      </p>

    </description>
  </image>
    </figure>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>3D-Vectors</title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Operations for three-dimensional vectors
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          GeoGebra3D tutorial
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-3d-vector-operations">
    <title>Operations and Properties</title>
    <p>
      The magnitude of a three-dimensional vector is calculated using the three-dimensional analog of the Pythagorean theorem. 
      <md>
        ||\mathbf{v}|| = \sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}
      </md>
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Magnitude</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          ||\langle 5, -2, 7 \rangle || = \sqrt{5^2 + (-2)^2 + 7^2} = \sqrt{78}
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Addition and subtraction is done component-wise just like in two-dimensions.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Addition and Subtraction</title>
      
      
      <p>
        <md>
        \langle 3, -6, 1 \rangle + \langle -4, 9, 2 \rangle = \langle -1, 3, 3 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
        \langle 3, -6, 1 \rangle - \langle -4, 9, 2 \rangle = \langle 7, -15, -1 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      One way to multiply with three-dimensional vectors is <em>scalar multiplication</em> where we multiply a vector by a scalar to "rescale" the vector. We will look at the other
      two ways to multiply with three-dimensional vectors.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>Scalar Multiplication</title>
      <p>
        <md>
          3\langle 7, -4, 8 \rangle = \langle 21, -12, 24 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \frac{1}{2}\langle 7, -4, 8 \rangle = \langle \frac{7}{2}, -2, 4 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md>
          -\langle 7, -4, 8 \rangle = \langle -7, -4, -8 \rangle
        </md>
        Multiplying a vector by negative makes it point in the opposite direction!
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      A useful scalar multiplication is to multiply it by 1/magnitude. This creates what is called a unit vector since the magnitude of the rescaled vector is 
      magnitude/magnitude = 1 unit.
    </p>
    <example>
      <title>A Unit Vector</title>
      <p>
        Given the vector <m>\langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle </m>, the magnitude is <m>||\langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle|| = \sqrt{14}</m>. Then the unit vector is:
        <md>
          \frac{1}{\sqrt{14}}\langle 2, -3, 1 \rangle = \langle \frac{2}{\sqrt{14}}, -\frac{3}{\sqrt{14}}, \frac{1}{\sqrt{14}}\rangle
        </md>
      </p>
          </example>

  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="geogebra-3d">
    <title>GeoGebra 3D</title>
    <p>
      We will close this section with a video tutorial of using GeoGebra 3D. If you click on the calculator icon at the top of the page, the GeoGebra 3D calculator will
      pop up in the lower right corner of your screen. Since we will be studying three dimensional space for the rest of the quarter and a standard graphing calculator does
      not have 3D capabilities, GeoGebra 3D is a good option for us to visualize objects in three-dimensional space. Desmos also has a 3D option and I will introduce another
      option in a future section.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>A Short Introduction to GeoGebra 3D (8 minutes).</caption>
      <video youtube="xK32uosH4us"/>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Here are three hour long webinar's from GeoGebra's official channel <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uw-Seln32qw" visual="Part 1"/>, <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WLlllUZdf1k" visual="Part 2"/>
      and <url href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=f96Dm01DoqY" visual="Part 3"/> if you want to do a deep dive.
    </p>
